Define W_EXITCODE where the platform does not.

widget.cc composes a wait status with W_EXITCODE(0, SIGKILL).  That macro
is a glibc/BSD extension, not POSIX, and QNX's <sys/wait.h> does not have
it:

    widget.cc:927:30: error: 'W_EXITCODE' was not declared in this scope

The encoding itself is the same.  QNX's own <sys/wait.h> defines
WEXITSTATUS(s) as ((s) >> 8) & 0xff and WTERMSIG(s) as (s) & 0x7f, so the
usual definition composes a status QNX's accessors read back correctly.
Guarded with #ifndef so any platform that does define it keeps its own.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/src/widget.cc
+++ b/src/widget.cc
@@ -22,6 +22,16 @@
 
 #include <sys/wait.h> // for W_EXITCODE
 
+#ifndef W_EXITCODE
+/* W_EXITCODE is a glibc/BSD convenience macro, not POSIX, and QNX does not
+ * define it.  QNX uses the same wait-status encoding, which its own <sys/wait.h>
+ * states directly: WEXITSTATUS is ((status) >> 8) & 0xff and WTERMSIG is
+ * (status) & 0x7f.  So composing a status is the same shift and or as everywhere
+ * else -- this is not a QNX-specific layout, just a missing spelling.
+ */
+#define W_EXITCODE(ret, sig) ((ret) << 8 | (sig))
+#endif
+
 #include <exception>
 #include <new>
 #include <stdexcept>
